/* book exam traps: Writing that IMA removes redundancy. Shared hardware creates common-mode faults, so critical functions are split across modules and cabinets. | Calling a circuit card an LRU. The LRU is the box swapped on the apron; cards inside it are shop replaceable units. | Confusing ARINC 653 (partitioning in software) with ARINC 429 or ARINC 664 (data on a wire). | Adding partition windows and forgetting that the total must fit inside one major frame. | Saying BITE proves the system healthy. It tests its own box; wiring and neighbouring units can still be at fault. */
